  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Promotion and Defence of Human Rights in El Salvador</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Despite the signing of the peace accords in El Salvador more than 15 years ago, peace-building and the defence of human rights continues to be a high priority for this small country that suffers disproportionate levels of violence. In the past, the lack of political will to address unresolved cases of torture, disappearances and massacres during the civil war stood as a major obstacle to achieving peace and reconciliation within Salvadoran society. However with the new FMLN government of Mauricio Funes and its stated commitment to build a just and democratic society, while respecting human rights, there is an unprecedented opportunity to advance significantly in the objectives of this project. This new political will faces challenges as El Salvador is affected by the wave of social violence common in many post war societies, which is fuelled by drug trafficking, economic hardship and lack of job opportunities for much of the population. The presence of youth gangs and organised crime syndicates continues to be a major contributing factor to the increase in social violence and delinquency. Feminicides (women&#x2019;s murders) have increased rampantly in the last years denoting a serious problem with gender-based violence.

To address this situation Tutela Legal&#x2019;s institutional program is promoting and defending human rights directed at the most vulnerable and violated sectors of society, women, men and children, who lack resources and possibilities to have their human rights respected.  Tutela&#x2019;s programme includes legal aid, advocacy, and organisational and educational support on human rights, especially to human rights parish commissions.  Investigative processes of human rights violations, training processes, promotion, consulting and organizational strengthening are part of the programme.  Information, assistance and support to this vulnerable population will be provided in a participatory manner, a process that allows beneficiaries to appropriate legal tools to strengthen their ability to become true subjects to transform their reality.  Processes will also be carried out for the formation of parish pastoral agents to prevent violence and to promote social harmony in nine municipalities that have been most affected by violence.</narrative>
  </description>
